# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 4 - Consensus Protocol
#
# Figure 4 specifies the binary consensus protocol over the
# Figure 3 round structure.  Each phase i comprises the paper's
# three steps, at rounds 3i+1, 3i+2 and 3i+3, each completed by
# Figure 3 delivering n-t validated messages.  On each completion
# event, Figure 4 updates the local value, may decide, and
# broadcasts for the next step.
#
# Round numbering here is the paper's, 1-based: phase i owns
# rounds 3i+1, 3i+2, 3i+3, and each step reads the messages of
# its OWN round.  The C is 0-based (phase i owns rounds 3i, 3i+1,
# 3i+2); bracha87Fig4ToC.dtc carries that conversion, mapping the
# step name to the C sub-round index.
#
# Per phase i:
#   Step 1 (round 3i+1):
#     value_p := majority of n-t validated round-(3i+1) values
#     (value_p := initial value for phase 0)
#   Step 2 (round 3i+2):
#     if more than n/2 round-(3i+2) messages have the same value v
#       then value_p := (d, v)
#       else value_p unchanged (non-deterministic across processes)
#   Step 3 (round 3i+3):
#     case (i)   more than 2t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d, v)
#                  agree on v: decide v
#     case (ii)  more than t (and at most 2t) such messages: value_p := v
#     case (iii) otherwise: value_p := coin_i
#     advance to phase i+1, step 1
#
# Bracha pitfall 1 (post-decide continuation): a process that has
# decided MUST continue broadcasting, with value_p := decision, until
# the surrounding application releases it.  The "have decided"
# guard in each step's update rule preserves the decision value
# while still outputting broadcast and advance side-effects.
#
# Predicates appearing as inputs are computed by C functions over
# the validated set delivered by Figure 3 (majority counts,
# d-flag tallies); see bracha87Fig4ToC.dtc for their bindings.
# The coin function for case (iii) is supplied by the surrounding
# application; the DTC selects the coin output, the C invokes it.
#
# Inputs (the situation when Figure 3 signals a round complete;
# the round names the step within its phase):
#   "step"                                                   { 1, 2, 3 }
#   "have decided"                                           { yes, no }
#   ">n/2 round-(3i+2) messages have same value v"           { yes, no }
#   ">2t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v"     { yes, no }
#   ">t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v"      { yes, no }
#
# Outputs:
#   "set value to majority of round (3i+1)"       { yes, no }
#   "set value to (d, majority of round (3i+2))"  { yes, no }
#   "decide v"                                    { yes, no }
#   "adopt v"                                     { yes, no }
#   "set value to coin flip"                      { yes, no }
#
# Two further actions - broadcast the value, advance to the next
# step - are implicit on every round completion event;
# they are not modeled as DTC outputs because they have no
# yes/no decision (they always fire here).  The C wrapper may
# attach implementation-level terminal handling (e.g. suppress
# broadcast at maxPhases limit) by gating them on additional
# state outside this file.

# ===== Step 1: set value to majority of round (3i+1) =====

# Rule 4.1.  At step 1, set value_p to the majority of the n-t
# validated round-(3i+1) values.  Skipped when already decided
# (the decision value is preserved).
@"set value to majority of round (3i+1)","step","have decided"
yes,1,no
no,1,yes
no,2,
no,3,

# ===== Step 2: set value to (d, majority of round (3i+2)) =====

# Rule 4.2.  At step 2, if more than n/2 of the round-(3i+2)
# messages have the same value v, set value_p := (d, v).  Otherwise
# value_p is unchanged.  Skipped when already decided.
@"set value to (d, majority of round (3i+2))","step","have decided",">n/2 round-(3i+2) messages have same value v"
yes,2,no,yes
no,2,no,no
no,2,yes,
no,1,,
no,3,,

# ===== Step 3 case (i): decide v =====

# Rule 4.3.  At step 3, if more than 2t of the round-(3i+3)
# messages of form (d, v) agree on v, decide v.  Skipped when
# already decided (decision is final; the second time through
# the same case the rule does not re-fire).
@"decide v","step","have decided",">2t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v"
yes,3,no,yes
no,3,no,no
no,3,yes,
no,1,,
no,2,,

# ===== Step 3 case (ii): adopt v =====

# Rule 4.4.  At step 3, if more than t (but not more than 2t)
# of the round-(3i+3) messages of form (d, v) agree on v, set
# value_p := v.  Skipped when already decided or when case (i)
# fired.
@"adopt v","step","have decided",">2t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v",">t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v"
yes,3,no,no,yes
no,3,no,yes,
no,3,no,no,no
no,3,yes,,
no,1,,,
no,2,,,

# ===== Step 3 case (iii): set value to coin flip =====

# Rule 4.5.  At step 3, if no more than t of the
# round-(3i+3) messages of form (d, v) agree on v, set
# value_p := coin_i.  Skipped when already decided or when case
# (i) or (ii) fired.
@"set value to coin flip","step","have decided",">2t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v",">t round-(3i+3) messages of form (d,v) agree on v"
yes,3,no,no,no
no,3,no,yes,
no,3,no,no,yes
no,3,yes,,
no,1,,,
no,2,,,
